  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 258 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Another shortwave is approaching the region early this \nmorning, bringing showers and thunderstorms to parts of northern New \nYork and Vermont. This wave is expected to move through during the \nday today, with better aligned forcing for our region than \nyesterday. Increasing diurnal instability around 1000-1500 J/kg \nfocused around the international border and across the Champlain \nValley with similar shear profiles to yesterday will bring better \nchances for thunderstorms to the region this afternoon, though a few \nshowers may be possible in the mid to late morning with little \ncapping inversion present. Some of the more robust high resolution \nmodels are projecting 2100-2300 J/kg of surface based CAPE, and \nfreezing levels have been lower than typical for this time of year. \nInstability will be highest in northern Vermont and the Champlain \nValley, though showers and thunderstorms anywhere across the \nnorthern portions of the CWA will be possible tomorrow. Brief gusty \nwinds around 40 mph and small hail will be possible. Precipitable \nwater values will slightly increase today to near an inch and a \nquarter, with parallel motion and training cells. However, again, \ndeep warm cloud depths will be thin with a low risk for any flooding \nconcerns. Temperatures today will likely remain on the cooler side \nwith highs in the 70s to around 80 F in northwesterly flow. \n\nTroughing will slide east with surface high pressure building in \nfrom Canada tonight and Friday. More calm and quiet conditions are \nexpected through Saturday night. High temperatures will be slightly \nbelow seasonal normals in the 70s and lower 80s. Nights will be on \nthe cooler side as well with prime radiational cooling conditions \npresent and lows in the mid 40s to mid 50s. Radiational fog looks \nincreasingly likely in the typical valley spots tonight and Friday \nnight, especially in locations that see rainfall today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Generally no real changes to the expectations for a good \nchance for widespread, beneficial rain late Sunday through Sunday \nnight. Probabilities of at least a half inch of rain have ticked up \nfrom 24 hours ago into the 30 to 50% range, while thunderstorm \nchances remain conditional on a more northward type of low track. \nAmong global models, all of the GEFS and GEPS membership shows CAPE \nremaining under 500 J/kg during this event, but a minority of ENS \nmembers are depicting a wedge of greater instability making \nnorthward progress into at least northern New York, suggesting at \nleast elevated thunderstorms would be possible, enhancing rainfall \nrates at times. Otherwise, we'll have to watch on Monday behind this \nlow pressure system if there is enough moisture to work with to have \nsome thunderstorm development as a cool front and trough follows \nbehind it. For now, greatest potential for thunderstorms Monday is \nin northeastern Vermont, but this portion of the forecast is rather \nuncertain given lack of a strong trigger similar to what we've seen \nin recent days in a fast flow pattern.\n\nTuesday continues to look largely dry associated with broad ridging. \nWednesday may start similarly, then take a turn towards more active \nweather with broadly good large scale support for organized showers \nand thunderstorms as the next trough moves in from the west. Given \nwe're seven days out and only weak signals for severe weather show \nup in the long range guidance, this is not likely to be a \nsignificant event but will bear watching if you have outdoor plans. \nSeasonable weather is anticipated through this period, with daytime \ntemperatures mainly in the 70s with dewpoints in the 50s, trending a \nonly a bit warmer and more humid on Wednesday.",
